Abhijit Menon-Sen writes:
>>  find( const String & ) depends on the casting operator from T to 
>>  String to deliver a search key.
>
> That is not how I intended it to work (though I might have 
> misunderstood things, and it actually does work that way). I wanted 
> to rely on classes that want this behavious defining an operator !=( 
> const String & ), and leaving casting entirely out of the picture.
>
> Can't it work that way?

Sure. In that case, operator!=() gets the, ah, innovative semantics. Our 
style guide says we don't do that.

>>  What are the three first places in which we'd use them?
>
> Mapping mailbox names to IDs (and thence via a Map to an object)?

Two more?

Btw, do we want to keep a tree of mailboxes, or a flat list? If a tree, 
then using LIST the way RFC2683 says works well, but we pay for that 
with some complexity.

